  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Sensors thousands of miles apart suddenly recorded the exact same noise. In the South Pacific, underwater sensors picked up an ultra-low-frequency sound echoing across the ocean. It was extraordinarily powerful, and it originated thousands of miles away.",
        "visual_query": "deep ocean underwater hydrophone sensors",
        "visual_fallback": "dark ocean water"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Scientists named the strange signal the Bloop. Its acoustic profile matched biological sounds, rising in pitch like a whale call. But the audio signature was vastly louder than any creature on Earth.",
        "visual_query": "sound wave frequency audio spectrogram",
        "visual_fallback": "glowing blue soundwave"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Could an undiscovered giant really be hiding in the dark abyss? For years, theorists speculated about colossal sea beasts. The math showed no living animal had vocal cords large enough to shake an ocean.",
        "visual_query": "deep sea trench dark abyss",
        "visual_fallback": "dark ocean depth"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The turn came when oceanographers deployed underwater sensors right along the Antarctic ice sheet. They finally matched the exact Bloop audio profile to a natural event: a massive glacial icequake. Colossal ice shelves were cracking and shearing apart.",
        "visual_query": "antarctic ocean ice shelf breaking",
        "visual_fallback": "melting iceberg ocean"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Shattering polar glaciers generate immense low-frequency energy that travels through deep ocean channels for thousands of miles. The Bloop wasn't a leviathan waking up\u2014it was the sound of Antarctica breaking.",
        "visual_query": "antarctica glacier cracking aerial view",
        "visual_fallback": "frozen ocean landscape"
      }
    ],
